  "course": {
    "narrative": "A 68-year-old gentleman with stage III CKD, CAD, HF, and BPH presented for nephrology consultation due to acute creatinine rise to 2.3. Potential etiologies included prerenal volume depletion from furosemide and weight loss, Chinese herbal supplement use causing interstitial nephritis, urinary tract obstruction from BPH, or TriCor interference. He also had orthostatic dizziness with low diastolic blood pressure and elevated CK (653) consistent with myositis from statin/fibrate therapy. Crestor was discontinued, atenolol, enalapril, and Lasix doses were reduced, and he was advised to avoid NSAIDs and herbal supplements.",
